{"kind":"stack","id":"s-classkeys","title":"Classification keys: grouping living things","theme":{"background":"#f4f9f1","foreground":"#1f3b1a","font":"Georgia, serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-q","content":"Which group does a frog belong to?"},{"type":"button","id":"c1-a","label":"Amphibians","why":"Frogs live on land and in water, so they are amphibians.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c1-b","label":"Mammals","why":"Pupils mix frogs up with mammals because both can be found near ponds.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c1-c","label":"Reptiles","why":"Pupils think frogs are reptiles because both live near water and have smooth skin.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-t","content":"Classification key"},{"type":"text","id":"c2-x","content":"A set of questions with yes or no answers that leads you to the name of a living thing."},{"type":"button","id":"c2-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"A key asks: Does it have feathers? Which group does a animal with feathers belong to?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"Birds","why":"Feathers are the feature that marks out birds.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"Fish","why":"Pupils think fish because both birds and fish live in water.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"Insects","why":"Pupils mix insects up with birds because both can fly.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-t","content":"You are exploring a pond. You spot a small creature with six legs. Do you catch it or record it in your notebook?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"Catch it","why":"","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"Record it","why":"","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c5-t","content":"You catch the creature gently and place it in a jar. Using your key, you find it is an insect."},{"type":"button","id":"c5-n","label":"Continue","why":"","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-t","content":"You sketch the creature carefully and use the key on your sheet. It leads you to the group insects."},{"type":"button","id":"c6-n","label":"Continue","why":"","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-q","content":"Which is the best first question to ask when using a classification key?"},{"type":"button","id":"c7-a","label":"Does it have a backbone?","why":"Backbone is an early split that separates big groups of animals.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c7-b","label":"What colour is it?","why":"Pupils choose colour first, but colour varies within one group.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c7-c","label":"How big is it?","why":"Pupils think size helps, but size can vary within one species.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-t","content":"Local environment"},{"type":"text","id":"c8-x","content":"The living things and places near your home or school, such as a hedge, a pond or a park."},{"type":"button","id":"c8-n","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-q","content":"Which living thing is a plant?"},{"type":"button","id":"c9-a","label":"Oak tree","why":"Trees make their own food using sunlight, so they are plants.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-b","label":"Mushroom","why":"Pupils think mushrooms are plants, but they are fungi.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c9-c","label":"Snail","why":"Pupils see snails in gardens, but a snail is an animal.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-q","content":"How many legs does an insect have?"},{"type":"button","id":"c10-a","label":"Six","why":"Insects always have six legs, a key feature of the group.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"Four","why":"Pupils count four legs for insects, mixing them up with spiders or small mammals.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c10-c","label":"Eight","why":"Pupils mix insects up with spiders, which have eight legs.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-q","content":"What is a key used for?"},{"type":"button","id":"c11-a","label":"Naming and grouping living things","why":"A key leads you step by step to the name and group of a living thing.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c12"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c11-b","label":"Measuring how fast animals move","why":"Pupils think a key measures speed, but a key only sorts living things.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c12"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c11-c","label":"Growing new plants","why":"Pupils confuse a key with a gardening guide, but keys only identify living things.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c12"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c12","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c12-h","content":"Well done: you can use classification keys!"},{"type":"text","id":"c12-m","content":"You explored living things near you and learned to group and name them. Keep looking closely at the world around you."}],"actions":[]}}
